      "message": "This logic is a significant deviation from the existing has_ghosting().  I do not believe it is acceptable.\n\nFor instance, if column 1 and 2 and row 1 and 2 are all registering as pressed (four coordinates), but (1, 1) does not correspond to an actual key, then on the first iteration, s would get a value with bit 2 set (since bit 1 is masked by actual_key_mask), on second iteration, r will have bit 1 and 2 set, and would then run foul of this check, since r has two bits, and the intersection with s is non-empty.\n\nYet, such three keys pressed, without any inconsistency due to non-atomic scanning, is something that the existing has_ghosting handles by interpreting as the three keys all being pressed, which is what we want.",
